mk("MjijB6ExetI","The Minifreak Vocoder is Awesome!",
 "vocoder-as-insert-fx-any-patch-carrier-mic-modulator-mode-decay-formant-dry-mic-slot-order-arp-square-lfo-formant",
 "Vocoder as an insert effect on any synth patch: the patch itself is the carrier and a microphone is the modulator, with mode (clean, vintage, narrow, gated), decay, formant (neutral or higher) and dry-mic controls, placement in the effects chain (before or after distortion and chorus), arpeggiated carriers and a slow square LFO on formant",
 "Turn any existing pad, bass or lead patch into a vocoder voice by putting the vocoder in an effect slot instead of building a special vocoder patch. The synth patch is the carrier (all its oscillators, voice mode, unison, envelopes and pitch bend still apply) and an external microphone is the modulator, so the patch goes silent until you speak or sing. Choose the vocoder mode (clean, vintage, narrow, gated; the presenter prefers clean), set decay (how long bands hold after the voice stops), set formant (neutral or higher; low settings sounded worse) and mix in some dry mic if wanted. Then experiment with where the vocoder sits in the chain (first, before distortion, with chorus after distortion, or replacing an EQ slot), add long reverb after it, run the arpeggiator so the vocoder speaks in rhythm, and modulate formant with a slow, deep square LFO. Rich carriers work best: e.g. a pluck oscillator plus a 2-operator FM oscillator, or a mono virtual-analog patch an octave down with filter envelope movement, chorus and distortion. Our first dedicated vocoder skill; the existing ones only borrow a 'vocode' spectral-warp mode.",
 ["Synth or DAW with a vocoder effect that accepts an external audio input (or a vocoder plugin with sidechain), or Web Audio: getUserMedia mic → bank of bandpass BiquadFilters + envelope followers controlling gains on the same bands of the synth carrier (implementation note)","Microphone","Distortion, chorus, reverb and an LFO/mod matrix"],
 [("carrier_patch","patch","Any existing synth patch (pad, bass, lead)",True),("modulator","audio","Live mic or recorded voice",True),("mode","enum","clean | vintage | narrow | gated",False),("formant","number","Neutral or higher",False)],
 [("vocoder_voice","patch","Patch with vocoder in its FX chain"),("fx_order","list","Final effects order, e.g. vocoder → distortion → chorus → reverb")],
 ["The carrier is silent without modulator input: check the mic before assuming the patch is broken.","Only one vocoder instance per patch in the source synth; if your host allows more, start with one anyway.","Product walkthrough; hardware mounting tip, patron shout-out and preset-pack names dropped. Values were given as positions (neutral, deep, slow), not numbers."],
 [("Start from an init patch or any existing preset. Put the vocoder (external input) in a free effect slot, replacing a less important effect such as EQ or delay if needed. Connect a microphone to the audio input.","vocoder slot","Playing keys alone is silent; speaking while holding keys produces the vocoded voice"),
  ("Pick the vocoder mode (clean, vintage, narrow, gated). Set decay for how long the bands hang on, formant at neutral or a bit higher, and the dry-mic amount to taste.","vocoder controls","Clear, intelligible speech with the synth's pitch"),
  ("Make the carrier rich: e.g. oscillator 1 a plucked-string type, oscillator 2 a 2-operator FM type; or a mono virtual-analog patch an octave down with filter-envelope movement. Use unison or mono as needed and play lead lines with pitch bend.","carrier design","Brighter, fuller carriers give more intelligible vocoding"),
  ("Try different slot positions: vocoder first, then distortion, then chorus after the distortion; or vocoder after a chorus and distortion carrier. Add a long reverb after.","fx order","Each order gives a noticeably different texture"),
  ("Turn on the arpeggiator with a held chord so the vocoded voice is re-triggered rhythmically.","arp carrier","Speech becomes a rhythmic vocal pattern"),
  ("Modulate formant with an LFO: square shape, slow rate, deep (even maximum) depth. Reduce unison spread if the result gets too smeared.","formant LFO","The vowel character flips between two formant positions in time")],
 ["With no mic input the patch is silent; with speech it is audible and pitched by the keys.","Changing formant from neutral to higher audibly shifts vowel character without changing played pitch.","Moving the vocoder before vs after distortion produces an audible difference in grit and intelligibility.","With the square LFO on formant, the voice alternates between two distinct timbres at the LFO rate."],
 ["vocoder","external-carrier","formant","fx-chain","arpeggiator","voice","web-audio"],
 note="Hardware walkthrough; mounting tip, patron and preset-pack talk dropped.")

mk("e1AMHtuuuTQ","STOP Using Stereo Reverb (Do This Instead) (Dub Techno Tutorial Mixing)",
 "mono-reverb-pockets-narrow-width-before-pingpong-wet-dry-as-distance-post-reverb-bandpass-lfo-dry-left-reverb-right",
 "Mono (narrowed) reverb as a mixing and sound-design tool: narrow each 100%-wet reverb into a placeable 'pocket', pan pockets to separate parts, put a stereo ping-pong delay after the width stage so the pocket travels left-right, use the wet/dry mix as a distance control, filter the reverb tail post-reverb with an LFO-moved bandpass, and split dry left / mono reverb right for pseudo-stereo",
 "Clean up a crowded ambient or electronic mix and get new textures by making reverbs mono or narrow instead of wide. For each sound, decide what stays stereo (e.g. a wide atmos bed with high-end detail) and what becomes a pocket: narrow the reverb width (it does not have to be fully mono; side-reducing imagers sound better than fold-to-mono), then pan the pocket to its own spot and turn it down, since a centred or narrow reverb is more audible and needs less level. Put a ping-pong delay after the width utility (low feedback, lowpass in the feedback path) so the mono pocket moves left and right without washing the whole field. Treat the wet/dry mix as a distance knob: fully wet is far, fully dry is close, which is more obvious with mono reverb. Filter after the reverb with a bandpass moved back and forth (manually or by LFO), with the filter mix keeping some high end, to shape the tail as its own sound. For pseudo-stereo, put a mono reverb on a send, pan the dry sound fully left and the reverb fully right, then bus both through a driven resonant filter. A fully wet mono reverb on an atmos track, sidechained, becomes a wall of sound that sits behind the lead.",
 ["DAW or Web Audio graph with reverb, stereo width/imager utility, panner, ping-pong delay and filters; in Web Audio: ConvolverNode → mono sum (ChannelMerger/Splitter or a mid-only gain) → StereoPannerNode → ping-pong delay pair (implementation note)","Sidechain compressor (optional)","LFO for filter movement"],
 [("tracks","list","Parts competing for space (atmos, steps, plucks, chords)",True),("width_per_reverb","number","0 = mono pocket … 100% = full stereo",True),("pan_positions","table","Where each pocket sits",True)],
 [("stereo_map","table","Per-track: stays stereo or pocket, width, pan, reverb level"),("pocket_fx_chains","patch","Reverb → width → pan → ping-pong delay → post filter chains")],
 ["Narrowing can be partial: keep a sound slightly wider if it serves the part, as long as it reads as one source.","Prefer side-reducing width control over hard mono summing to avoid phase cancellation.","If vocals are the main element, the presenter suggests stereo reverb on them; mono reverb suits chops, drums, claps and hats.","No numbers were given apart from 100% wet and full-left/full-right pans. Set the rest by ear. Plugin names not needed."],
 [("Solo through the parts and decide which stays stereo (e.g. an atmos bed with high-end detail through a moving notch filter into a 100% wet reverb) and which become mono/narrow pockets.","stereo plan","A list of stereo vs pocket tracks"),
  ("For a pocket track, set its reverb 100% wet (on the insert) and narrow its width with a side-reducing imager or width utility, partly or fully.","narrow reverb","The reverb becomes a focused, one-dimensional pocket"),
  ("Pan each pocket to its own spot (e.g. short step pocket right, layered second step slightly wider and left, main part centre) and turn levels down: narrow reverbs are more audible, so they need less.","place pockets","Parts stop fighting; the mix gets clearer at lower levels"),
  ("Put the ping-pong delay after the width stage, with low feedback and a lowpass on the feedback, so the mono pocket is carried left and right.","delay after width","A narrow sound that still moves across the stereo field"),
  ("Use the reverb mix as a distance control: fully wet = far behind, fully dry = right in front. Shave highs for extra depth.","distance","Front-to-back placement by one knob"),
  ("Place a bandpass filter after the reverb and move it back and forth by hand or with an LFO; use the filter's mix to keep some high end. Post-reverb filtering shapes the tail as its own sound.","post-reverb filter","The reverb shadow becomes a moving, organic texture"),
  ("Pseudo-stereo: send the sound to a mono reverb return, pan the dry sound hard left and the reverb hard right, then route both to a bus with a driven, resonant filter (cut lows, shorten the reverb, add movement).","dry/wet split","Two mono sources that together sound wide"),
  ("For a background wall: put a fully wet mono reverb on the atmos track and sidechain it to the lead or kick so it ducks out of the way.","wall of sound","A dense bed that sits behind the main part")],
 ["After narrowing, the pocket can be moved with the pan control and is heard as one spot.","With the delay placed after the width utility, repeats alternate left and right while the reverb itself stays narrow.","Sweeping the reverb mix from dry to wet makes the source move audibly from front to back.","The dry-left / reverb-right pair sounds wide on speakers but collapses to a single source in mono without cancellation."],
 ["mono-reverb","stereo-width","mixing","ping-pong-delay","depth","post-reverb-filter","ambient","web-audio"],
 note="Mixing tutorial; end-screen promotion dropped.")
